{"step_index":371,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-08-20T16:10:16Z","content":"To get **SnapDialer** running live on your iPhone for beta testing, here are the **2 clean pathways** (depending on whether you want instant testing via Expo Go or a standalone installable app):\n\n---\n\n```\n┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│                       THE 2 BETA TESTING PATHWAYS                           │\n├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│                                                                             │\n│  PATHWAY A: The 60-Second Tunnel (Fastest — Uses Expo Go)                   │\n│  • Bypasses all Microsoft Defender, VPN, and router Wi-Fi isolation issues. │\n│  • Connects through a secure cloud relay to your iPhone.                    │\n│  • Takes ~60 seconds to launch.                                             │\n│                                                                             │\n│  PATHWAY B: EAS Preview Build (Standalone App on Your iPhone)               │\n│  • Installs \"SnapDialer\" directly onto your iPhone home screen.             │\n│  • Runs independently — no computer or terminal connection needed.          │\n│                                                                             │\n└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n```\n\n---\n\n## Pathway A: The 60-Second Tunnel (Recommended for Instant Testing)\n\nSince Microsoft Defender on your phone previously blocked direct local Wi-Fi port `8081`, we use a **cloud relay tunnel** to bridge your PC to your iPhone effortlessly.\n\n### Step 1: Open Command Prompt / PowerShell on your PC\nIn your `snap-dialer` directory, run:\n```cmd\ncd C:\\Users\\brorm\\.gemini\\antigravity\\scratch\\snap-dialer\nnpx localtunnel --port 8081\n```\n*(This gives you a public URL like `https://funny-tiger-42.loca.lt`)*\n\n### Step 2: Start Expo with that Hostname\nIn a second terminal window, run:\n```cmd\ncd C:\\Users\\brorm\\.gemini\\antigravity\\scratch\\snap-dialer\nnpx expo start --port 8081\n```\n\n### Step 3: Open on iPhone\n1. Open the **Expo Go** app on your iPhone.\n2. Under **\"Enter URL manually\"**, enter the URL from Step 1 (e.g. `exp://funny-tiger-42.loca.lt`).\n3. The ValuTeachers dialer loads onto your phone!\n\n---\n\n## Pathway B: Standalone EAS Beta Build (True App Install)\n\nIf you want SnapDialer installed directly on your iPhone as a real standalone app icon (like from the App Store) that you can take with you to school visits without needing your PC:\n\n### Step 1: Log in to Expo (Free Account)\n```cmd\nnpx eas login\n```\n\n### Step 2: Build the iOS Preview App\n```cmd\nnpx eas build --profile preview --platform ios\n```\nEAS builds the iOS package in the cloud and outputs a **QR code / download link** in your terminal. You scan it with your iPhone camera to install **SnapDialer** directly to your home screen!\n\n---\n\n## Pre-Flight Checklist for Your First Test Session:\n\n1. **Google Gemini API Key (Optional):**\n   - If you want to snap **new physical paper sheets** using your camera, open **Settings** (top right gear icon) inside the app and paste your Gemini API key.\n   - If you don't enter a key, the built-in **ValuTeachers offline demo extractor** and the pre-loaded **Joseph Adona (VOL)** card are ready to test immediately!\n2. **Caller Profile Signature:**\n   - In Settings, set your name (e.g., *\"Alex\"*) so follow-up SMS templates auto-populate: *\"Hi Joseph, this is Alex with ValuTeachers...\"*\n3. **Try the ValuTeachers HUD Flow:**\n   - Tap **Launch Power Dialer** on the Volusia list.\n   - Tap **\"View Original Report Card\"** to inspect the proof modal.\n   - Swipe right on the contact card to trigger a test call via your iPhone's native dialer!\n\nWhich pathway would you like to run first? (I can guide you through either one step-by-step!)","thinking":"**Reviewing Beta Access**\n\nI'm currently reviewing the steps taken when the user tried connecting their phone. I've noted they're on a Windows PC. The next step is to examine the phone details to understand the connection attempt's context. I'm focusing on the phone setup and how it interacted with the PC environment. The goal is to identify what needs addressing to enable the beta testing.\n\n\n**Identifying Testing Pathways**\n\nI've got two viable pathways for live iPhone testing today, focused on minimal friction. The first, using Cloudflare Tunnel or `localtunnel`, leverages a secure, global HTTPS URL, completely bypassing Defender and router restrictions, offering the simplest setup. The second path involves an EAS Preview build for a direct install, which will be the longer path to take.\n\n\n"}
